1
00:00:01,033 --> 00:00:06,265
Here are two real examples of Sora using Parallel
MCP tools. Web Search finds information

2
00:00:06,265 --> 00:00:11,498
across the internet. Web Fetch reads the content at
a specific URL. We will verify

3
00:00:11,498 --> 00:00:13,591
the tool used for each request.

4
00:00:15,387 --> 00:00:20,541
In MCP settings, Parallel must be connected and
available to Sora. Web Search and Web

5
00:00:20,541 --> 00:00:25,696
Fetch must both be enabled. Each switch controls one
tool, and changes take effect on

6
00:00:25,696 --> 00:00:26,727
the next message.

7
00:00:27,793 --> 00:00:33,391
First, ask Sora to search the web for the official
Model Context Protocol documentation. This

8
00:00:33,391 --> 00:00:38,989
request supplies a topic. Parallel Web Search finds
relevant pages and returns results that Sora

9
00:00:38,989 --> 00:00:40,855
can use in its answer.

10
00:00:57,302 --> 00:01:02,929
The completed activity names Parallel search MCP and
Web Search. This confirms that Sora called

11
00:01:02,929 --> 00:01:08,181
the external search tool. The answer provides the
official website and a short explanation.

12
00:01:09,968 --> 00:01:15,240
Next, start a new conversation and give Sora a
specific page URL. Ask it to

13
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:20,512
use Parallel Web Fetch and summarize the retrieved
content. This request reads the selected page

14
00:01:20,512 --> 00:01:22,270
rather than searching for pages.

15
00:01:24,178 --> 00:01:29,292
The completed activity now names Web Fetch. Sora has
retrieved the page through Parallel and

16
00:01:29,292 --> 00:01:34,407
used the returned content to write its summary. Both
examples were executed live through Portage.

17
00:01:35,430 --> 00:01:40,533
Use Web Search when you have a question or topic.
Use Web Fetch when you

18
00:01:40,533 --> 00:01:45,635
already have a URL. In both cases, check the
completed tool activity, and control access

19
00:01:45,635 --> 00:01:48,356
using the individual tool switches in MCP settings.
